From maurice@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca Tue Jul 15 20:12:56 1997
Message-ID: <3.0.1.32.19970715180925.0084ba80@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca>
Date: Tue, 15 Jul 1997 18:09:25 -0600
From: Maurice W. Hilarius <maurice@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca>
Subject: Re: fdisk installing redhat alpha
In-Reply-To: <Pine.LNX.3.95.970715173934.14164A-100000@dns.cdb.edu.sv>

At 05:45 hrs. 07-15-97 -0600, you wrote:
>Thanks! i have passed this problem now, but i have found another one :<
>I got 2 disk: 1 GB, and i have partioned this one without problems, but i
>got another disk: 4 GB and i can set the number of sectors to the real one
>(BSD Disklabel report 113 and fdisk only allow a maximun of 63)???
>please... don't tell me that fdisk or ext2fs does not support my 4GB HD :(
>Hoping someone could help me.Thanks in advance.
Write down the numbers for heads,cylinders, sectors per track.
multiply them together. Heads x Cyl x SPT = x
Divide x by 63, divide this by 128, write down the result. 
Use this number for cylinders, and 63 and 128 for the other factors.
Go into fdisk, type x for expert mode, help to see the commands, use the
numbers you've now got.
Return from expert mode to the previous menu.
Partition away.
This works fine. SCSI controllers and disks use logical remapping anyway,
so the numbers are a convention, not a necessity.

Have fun!
BTW, when mke2fs'ing, if it gives you grief on big partitions, increase
your memory freepages.

Cheers,
	Maurice

