The little "riser" card holding the SCSI controller and connectors, *can*
support the standard size cable as well as the mini size *and the external
port, in one of its configurations. If you have one with only the mini and
external on it, you'd have to choose external or 3.5 internal, unless you
could find a cable to go from mini to standard size. See one of the Alpha
resellers, like DCG or Harddata, who post fairly often to the net.

> 2) Many folks have said that 3.5" drives produce too much heat for the UDB
> and will run the risk of overheating your machine. I wanted to know what
> the heat differences were on average between 2.5" drives and 3.5" drives.
> Also, are the speed differences that much different between 2.5" and 3.5"
> drives? Also, does anyone have an actual 3.5" scsi hard drive in their UDB?

Yes, 3.5s could generate a lot more heat, but exactly how much varies
greatly with the drive. And yes, 3.5s are *much* faster than the 2.5s.
I've a 233MHz version with 1Gb internal 3.5 SCSI (Quantum?), and it seems
fine, although I've no external port on that box, because of the riser
card configuration... :-(

> 4) I know that the UDB has a PCI slot. I as wondering if it was possible to
